{"mode": "explain_at_tier", "concept": "information-hiding", "target_tier": "Apply", "content": "Short answer: no — `private` alone doesn't settle it, and returning `internalRecords` directly does not preserve the storage abstraction.

Why: `private` is a visibility mechanism; information hiding is the design decision about what stays changeable behind the boundary. A `private` field whose value is handed out raw still exposes the representation. If `internalRecords` is a `List`, returning it gives callers a live handle to the very object the module stores — their `add`/`remove` mutates module state, and every caller now depends on "the records are stored in a mutable List." Swap that for an array, a map, or a database and many classes break, which is the course's test for a leaked secret: if the decision changes, more than one class must be edited.

So the fix isn't a different keyword on the field. It's to stop returning the internal object at all: hand back something that carries the data without carrying your storage decision (a copy, or better, domain operations like `recordCount()` / `findRecord(id)` instead of a raw collection). Which of those fits depends on what callers actually need to do with the records — that choice is yours to make, and I'll check your reasoning rather than write the line for you.", "sources": ["SEBook/designprinciples/informationhiding.md — https://github.com/tobiasduerschmid/tobiasduerschmid.github.io/blob/24db6bf0d4e4ebfa1331e6c772e1844cbaf2d9aa/SEBook/designprinciples/informationhiding.md", "SEBook/tools/java.md — https://github.com/tobiasduerschmid/tobiasduerschmid.github.io/blob/24db6bf0d4e4ebfa1331e6c772e1844cbaf2d9aa/SEBook/tools/java.md"], "coverage_gap": "The retrieved course passages classify a getter that returns an internal mutable collection as a representation leak and name "replace the exposed type with a domain object / expose domain operations" as the fix, but they do not state a language-specific copy or unmodifiable-view idiom, so no exact return statement is quoted from the material."}
